# Track what cannot be REGENERATED. Everything listed here can be.
#
# The test is not "is it output" but "would recovering it need a re-solve".
# Meshes are a pure function of geometry.py and its arguments, and gmsh is
# deterministic — E0e and E1c both confirmed identical inputs give a
# byte-identical mesh — so a mesh is recoverable from tracked source.
#
# ⚠️ NO TRAILING COMMENTS. gitignore treats '#' as a comment ONLY at the start
# of a line, so "*.msh  # big" is a literal pattern that matches nothing. The
# first draft of this file had exactly that and would have admitted 5 GB of
# meshes. Every pattern below was checked with `git check-ignore` against real
# filenames.

# regenerable from tracked source, and enormous
*.msh
*.meta.json
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# generated by solveconf.py from a tracked mesh sidecar
# narrow patterns only: a bare *.json would swallow baselines.json and every
# result file, which are the evidence for every claim in FINDINGS
e0*.json
e1*.json
r1*.json
!*.result.json
!*.criteria.json
!*.reference.json
!*.exact.json
!*.sweep.json

# 🔴 postpro/ IS PER-PROGRAMME, and assuming otherwise nearly cost 554 MB.
# resonance/ solves EIGENMODE: output is eig.csv, ~1 MB total, and recovering it
# costs hours — so it is tracked. waveguide/ solves DRIVEN sweeps that write
# thousands of frequency samples per solve: 554 MB, and every number in it is
# summarised in the *.result.json files that ARE tracked.
experiments/waveguide/postpro/

# waveguide rig logs: 211 MB, dominated by driven sweeps printing thousands of
# frequency samples that also live in postpro/. The SMALL ones are kept by the
# negations below because they carry declared criteria.
experiments/waveguide/*.log
!experiments/waveguide/r9*.log
!experiments/waveguide/r10*.log

# ignition/ is the superseded alumina-ring route: source and findings are
# history worth keeping, its meshes and solver output are not
experiments/ignition/postpro/
experiments/ignition/*_p.log

# mesh cache: byte-identical copies of meshes, keyed on the resolved geometry
# parameters plus the sha256 of geometry.py. Pure reuse of regenerable output,
# and the largest directory in the tree once it warms up. mesh.msh inside it is
# already covered by *.msh, but entry.json and meta.json are not.
meshcache/

# scratch
*.bak
*.bak[0-9]
*.corrupt

# deliberately NOT ignored:
#   postpro/    ~1 MB in resonance; recovering it costs hours of solving
#   *_p.log     solver convergence diagnostics
#   *.log       rig logs carry the criteria DECLARED BEFORE each run — a
#               re-run cannot reproduce a prediction, only a measurement

# 🔴 ops/queue.sh's OWN STATE — remote, not a rig log, and NOT synced.
# `rsync.sh` pushes everything not ignored here, and `ops/fetch.sh` pulls
# `*.log`. So QUEUE.log came down to the working tree and every subsequent sync
# pushed the STALE copy back over the RUNNING queue's record — silently erasing
# the entry it was in the middle of. Observed 2026-08-29: h3-aspect-02 was
# running while QUEUE.log's last line still read "QUEUE DONE ran 1/1" for the
# previous batch.
# ⚠️ QUEUE.list survives the same treatment only by luck: rsync replaces via a
# temp-and-rename, so a driver holding it open on fd 3 keeps reading the old
# inode. Do not rely on that.
experiments/resonance/QUEUE.log
experiments/resonance/QUEUE.list
